meta=None content=[TextContent(type='text', text='{"markdown": "** Suits** led Nielsen\\u2019s Top 10 most streamed programs of 2023 with a\\n\\n**total watch time of 57.7 billion minutes**. In the nine seasons that\\n\\n*Suits*aired, Harvey Specter (\\n\\n**Gabriel Macht**) ruled the hearts of millions of people across the world. Harvey is far from perfect. He\'s also far from real. But his winning attitude and confidence haven\'t failed to motivate anyone. The fictional character is relentless, talented beyond belief, and doesn\'t take no for an answer. He might be a little arrogant, but he makes up for it with his quick-witted humor and strong principles.\\n\\nHarvey is tailed within an inch of his life and has dropped great quotes and one-liners like only a few other characters in films and TV shows. Even when other characters like Mike Ross (**Patrick Adams**) and Rachel Zane (**Megan Markle**) left the show, Harvey\'s fandom remained unwavering, a testament to the fact that Macht\'s performance as Harvey is unrivaled. Even in the first eight seasons, Harvey\'s witty remarks and invincible quotes contribute to making the show one of the **best legal dramas on television**.\\n\\n**Image:** [Image file not found: https://static0.colliderimages.com/wordpress/wp-content/uploads/sharedimages/2025/06/03113358_poster_w780.jpg?q=70&fit=contain&w=480&dpr=1]\\n\\n##### Suits\\n\\n-\\n**Release Date** - 2011 - 2019\\n-\\n**Network** - USA Network\\n-\\n**Showrunner** - Aaron Korsh\\n-\\n**Directors** - Anton Cropper, Michael Smith, Christopher Misiano, Kevin Bray, Roger Kumble, Silver Tree, Cherie Nowlan, John Scott, Gabriel Macht, Patrick J. Adams, Emile B. Levisetti, F\\u00e9lix Enr\\u00edquez Alcal\\u00e1, Valerie Weiss, James Whitmore Jr., Jennifer Getzinger, Kate Dennis, Terry McDonough, Maurice Marable, Adam Davidson, David Platt, Dennie Gordon, Gregor Jordan, Julian Holmes, Kate Woods\\n\\n### Cast\\n\\n-\\n\\nGabriel MachtHarvey Specter -\\n\\nRick HoffmanLouis Litt\\n\\n-\\n**Genres** - Drama\\n\\n## 25 \\"I think that says it all, don\\u2019t you?\\"\\n\\n### \\"Trick of the Trade\\" - Season 1, Episode 6 (2011)\\n\\nWhile **Harvey is loved by Suits fans** as the effortlessly cool and ever-composed, quick-talking lawyer that he is, he has a certain mean streak to him as well that has led to some of his most famous quips. This is seen in the Season 1 episode \\u201cTrick of the Trade\\u201d when he abruptly shuts down an eager an enthusiastic young co-worker who bounces at the opportunity to offer Harvey a helping hand, Harvey initially responds with \\u201cWell, Alan\\u2026\\u201d.\\n\\nWhen the buoyant yet inexperienced lawyer rushes to correct Harvey by informing him his name is actually Aaron, Harvey\\u2019s coy and callous reply serves as a rather unambiguous message of his disinterest in Aaron\\u2019s offer. **It is as sharp as it is disrespectful, and a gem of Harvey\\u2019s slicing wit** that defined him in Season 1 yet was gradually eased as the series went on. It remains one of his most brutal moments.\\n\\n## 24 \\"Never answer the phone in front of a client.\\"\\n\\n### \\"Bail Out\\" - Season 1, Episode 5 (2011)\\n\\nMany of Harvey\\u2019s best and most memorable moments have come around due to his in-depth knowledge of how to work in a way that appeases his clients and keeps them coming back to him when they are in need. Often, in Season 1 particularly, this trait of his is best exemplified through his mentorship of Mike and his desire to make the cunning young fraud a sharp and capable lawyer.\\n\\nSome of these lessons come in funnier ways than others, such as when **he calls Mike when he knows he is meeting with a client and, upon Mike\\u2019s answering of his phone, immediately berates him** for it. The moment has plenty of comic levity within it, but it also serves as an essential lesson for Mike to not only ensure he places his full focus on his clients, but to make sure they realize he is doing so as well.\\n\\n## 23 \\"I know you\\u2019re not married. Still funny.\\"\\n\\n### \\"Bail Out\\" - Season 1, Episode 5 (2011)\\n\\nWhile Harvey Specter may have mellowed out in terms of his general conduct to his colleagues as *Suits *went on, one thing that always remained was **his playful yet barbed hostility towards his venomous and vindictive co-worker, Louis Litt **(**Rick Hoffman**). Even though the two do form a bond of mutual respect in time, seeing their rivalry\\u2019s most heated and piercing moments in the earlier seasons is a treat to watch, if only to revel in Harvey\\u2019s delightful cattiness.\\n\\nIn \\u201cBail Out,\\u201d the two engage in a verbal scrap that sees Litt remark that Specter smells of jealousy, a quip that invites Harvey to retort by saying that the aroma is actually Louis\\u2019 wife\\u2019s perfume. A somewhat stunned Louis can only watch on as **Harvey grins and twists the knife further**, saying \\u201cI know you\\u2019re not married. Still funny.\\u201d just to ensure he gets the absolute last laugh.\\n\\n## 22 \\"What are your choices when someone puts a gun to your head?\\"\\n\\n### \\"Errors and Omissions\\" - Season 1, Episode 2 (2011)\\n\\nComing in just *Suits*\\u2019 second episode, **Harvey\\u2019s philosophical lesson about leverage and blackmail is one of the earliest lessons he taught Mike in their relationship**, as well as one of the most important in the context of navigating the cut-throat world of law firm politics. With Mike being boxed into a corner by Louis Litt, and seemingly being forced to do whatever his malignant colleague demands of him, Harvey imparts his wisdom on Mike with immediate results.\\n\\nWhile Mike initially admits defeat and says he just has to do whatever Louis wants of him, Harvey rebukes such an idea, telling Mike that there is no limit to what he can do to gain some dirt over Louis to level the playing field. It is a brilliant example of Harvey\\u2019s dogged determination to not be defeated, his cut-throat philosophy that encourages out-of-the-box thinking and bold schemes, especially in the most desperate circumstances.\\n\\n## 21 \\"I didn\\u2019t ask you to explain the problem to me. I asked you to fix it for me.\\"\\n\\n### \\"Identity Crisis\\" - Season 1, Episode 8 (2011)\\n\\nIn the Season 1 episode \\u201cIdentity Crisis,\\u201d Harvey and Mike are working on a case together and Harvey tells his prot\\u00e9g\\u00e9 to take charge of the issue at hand. **While Mike is fiercely intelligent**\\u2014as evidenced by his elaborate and detailed yet efficient breakdown of the case at hand\\u2014he is still overcoming a certain reluctance to really take initiative, something that Harvey aspires to work out of him.\\n\\nWithout mincing his words, Harvey sternly tells Mike that he wasn\\u2019t asked to explain the problem at hand, but to find a way to resolve it. **It reinforces to Mike that Harvey is not a man who is easily impressed, nor one who is nurturing of mistakes**, and has expectations for his application that will be tested. It\\u2019s a moment where Harvey throws Mike in the deep end and forces him to learn for himself without guidance or a safety net.\\n\\n## 20 \\u201cI\\u2019d say the ball\\u2019s in your court, but the truth is your balls are in my fist.\\u201d\\n\\n### \\"Pilot\\" - Season 1, Episode 1 (2011)\\n\\n**Pilot episodes are all about making strong first impressions**, and Harvey Specter was able to do just that when he shut down an argumentative opponent in *Suits*\\u2019 first ever episode. **The line is quintessentially Harvey, mixing an exuberant and gallivanting confidence with smooth wordplay and a touch of crude humor** to make a point that is both hysterically funny and devastatingly unambiguous.\\n\\nMacht embraces his character\\u2019s icy and suave demeanor as he rises from his chair to deliver the barbed blow and effectively silence posturing businessman Gerald Tate (**John Bedford Lloyd**). Complemented by Harvey\\u2019s wry and insincere apology for his rather visual metaphor, the quip is the best and most efficient line from the series pilot, one that immediately informed audiences exactly what kind of lovable scoundrel Harvey Specter would be going forward.\\n\\n## 19 \\"I don\\u2019t get lucky. I make my own luck.\\"\\n\\n### \\"Know When to Fold \\u2018Em\\" - Season 3, Episode 15 (2014)\\n\\nAn efficient and impressive penultimate episode to the third season of *Suits*, \\u201cKnow When to Fold \\u2018Em\\u201d sees the firm attacked by a former partner while Mike is forced to decide between keeping his job or being rid of his fraudulent guilt, and Harvey finds himself being extorted by rival lawyer James Quelling (**Damon Gupton**). When their rivalry comes to a head, Harvey drops one of the most pointed lines about how he operates.\\n\\nConfronting Quelling as a bit of a gloating victor, Harvey disposes of any notion that he survived Quelling\\u2019s attacks against him through sheer luck, **suggesting that he is always positioning himself to capitalize on any change in any situation**. The quote highlights why Harvey is not only such a beloved character, but why he is such an effective lawyer as well.\\n\\n## 18 \\"I don\\u2019t see any Toms or Harrys here.\\"\\n\\n### \\"Discovery\\" - Season 2, Episode 4 (2012)\\n\\nMany of Harvey Specter\\u2019s best one-liners have come as witty japes delivered at the expense of his temperamental and sometimes treacherous colleague, **Rick Hoffman\'s Louis Litt**. While these barbed quips are sometimes spoken behind Litt\\u2019s back, the Season 2 episode \\u201cDiscovery\\u201d sees Harvey coolly uttering one of his best verbal jabs straight to Louis\\u2019 face. The episode at large sees an under-siege Harvey offloading Mike to Louis, with the newly forged duo working well together as Harvey battles an old lawsuit.\\n\\nAfter barging into Harvey\\u2019s office, Louis berates him for his lack of care, stating that with no gatekeeper, any \\u201cTom, Dick, or Harry\\u201d could walk straight into his office. **Harvey\\u2019s retort is perfect, and Macht delivers the line with such an effortless swagger** that Louis\\u2019 exaggerated urgency is immediately diffused. As far as effective and hilarious lines go, it marks one of Harvey\\u2019s best in the series.\\n\\n## 17 \\"That wasn\\u2019t his cat.\\"\\n\\n### \\"All In\\" - Season 2, Episode 6 (2012)\\n\\nAnother example of Harvey gleefully ridiculing his workplace frenemy Louis Litt, \\u201cAll In\\u201d sees the suave lawyer and Jessica Pearson exchanging stories about their mutual colleague in the privacy of Jessica\\u2019s office. Their conversation on the matter begins with Jessica conceding that she may have had something to do with a past discretion that befell Louis that she let Harvey take the blame for.\\n\\nA touch despondent, Harvey admits that he probably deserved Louis\' ire for something else he had committed. He makes mention of a past incident where **Louis was irritated that his beloved cat has urinated in the corner of his office, then admits \\u201cthat wasn\\u2019t his cat.\\u201d** Jessica chortles, audiences laugh at Louis\\u2019 expense (again), and Harvey again emerges as the slick and suave schemer ever in control, making it a gem of a line that, perhaps crudely, embodies Harvey\\u2019s mischievous side.\\n\\n## 16 \\"Let me give you the ABCs of me\\u2026\\"\\n\\n### \\"One-Two-Three Go\\u2026\\" - Season 4, Episode 1 (201\\n\\nWhile he only appeared in a handful of episodes, Logan Sanders (**Brendan Hines**) is one of the **sharpest written villains *** Suits *has to offer. There is a certain depth to him, but he remains utterly contemptible, something that makes it so rewarding when Harvey snaps at him in the Season 4 premiere, \\u201cOne-Two-Three Go..\\u201d. When the wealthy inheritor of an industry company starts making demands of Harvey, the lawyer quickly sets to record straight.\\n\\nGiving him the basic rules of working with him, Harvey insists that he doesn\\u2019t attend meetings at the behest of anyone and his respect has to be earned, not demanded. The whole exchange is **a divine highlight of Harvey\\u2019s snappy wit meeting his more serious insistence on being treated right**, and the fact that Logan Sanders is on the receiving end of it makes it all the more sweet."}', annotations=None)] isError=False

